  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1202 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nMixture of rain, rain mixed with snow, and snow possible across\nWest Central Texas on Tuesday.\n\nAs mentioned yesterday, the chance for wintry precipitation seems\nto be increasing across at least portions of the area on Tuesday.\nThe RRFS started the trend yesterday, but most of the models now\nare showing at least some snow potential across areas north of a\nSan Angelo to Abilene line. This develops as a sharp upper level\nshortwave moves across the area, producing at least a medium\nchance (40-60%) of precipitation. Sounding aloft is a snow\nsounding with readings below zero, but near surface temperatures\nwill initially be too warm. However, as wet bulbing (evaporative \ncooling) develops low level temperatures will cool and the \npossibility of snow making it to the ground increases. Best \nchances will still be across the higher elevations from the \ncentral and western Concho Valley north into the Big Country, \nalthough a few snow flakes into San Angelo and Abilene is \npossible. At this point, given the relatively light precip, \ntemperatures staying above freezing, and warm ground temperatures \ndo not expect snow to stick very well. A dusting on some grassy \nareas will be possible. Still, little impact on roadways is \nexpected.\n\nModel blends showed highs on Tuesday in the upper 40s to near 50\nbut this seems overly optimistic given the clouds and precip\nacross the area. Lowered highs 3-5 degrees and brought most areas\ndown into the low to mid 40s. That may be still be too high and\nactual temperatures for the afternoon may end up in the upper 30s\nand lower 40s instead. Will take highs down even lower if current\ntrends continue.\n\nSystem shifts east and surface high tries to build into the area\nfor Wednesday morning. This would allow a decent radiational\ncooling set up to develop, both Wednesday and Thursday mornings.\nBlends may still be a little too warm and would not be surprised\nto see some temperatures down in the 20s one of these mornings and\nappropriate freeze warnings may be needed.\n\nAfternoon highs will start to climb after Tuesday, reaching back\ninto the mid 50s for Thanksgiving and then into the 60s to near 70\nfor the weekend.",
